<decompilertest>
<!--
    switchselector: record a lowered-switch cascade only when the switch value is
    computed inside the function, leaving every parameter-dispatch cascade as the
    compiler lowered it.

    `loweredswitch` (DIV-4, default on) DETECTS a compiler-lowered comparison
    cascade on the simplified, SSA'd CFG and then INSTALLS the BRANCHIND +
    JumpTable pre-SSA, on the re-lifted raw p-code of a restart.  Both renderings
    are correct C over the real switch value; which one reads better is a
    judgement call, and this option is the knob.

    Stage model: S2 "switch-model" sub-stage (the same sub-stage `loweredswitch`
    itself owns).  P0 assertion: `option switchselector on|off`.

    Layout (x86:LE:32, flat at 0x100000) - the Win32 callback shape, reduced to
    the dispatch:

      dlgproc @ 0x100000 :  push ebp; mov ebp,esp
                            mov  eax,[ebp+0xc]     ; the message: a STACK parameter
                            cmp  eax,0x111 / ja default / je 0x111
                            cmp  eax,0x10  / je ...
                            cmp  eax,0x2b  / je ...
                            cmp  eax,0x110 / jne default
                            (five one-line case bodies)

    Pass 1 (option switchselector on): the record is declined because the message
    is a parameter, not a value this function computes, and the compiler's own
    if/else-if chain over it survives.
    Pass 2 (option switchselector off - the shipped default): the cascade is
    re-rolled into a `switch` over that same parameter.

    The ON pass runs FIRST on purpose.  The lowered-switch hint store is sticky
    across `clear()` by design (the detect half records on one pass, the install
    half consumes it on the restart), so a record left by an earlier pass would be
    installed by a later one whatever the option then says.  Declining leaves no
    record, so `on` then `off` is the ordering in which each pass really re-runs
    the detection.

    Asserts #1/#2 pin the recovered dispatch to pass 2 alone and #3 pins the
    surviving chain to pass 1 alone.  #4 is the regression pin: the switch
    dispatches on the message parameter, never on a constant.  #5 pins the
    parameter into BOTH prototypes and #6 a case body into both bodies, so the
    option is shown to change the structure and not the content.
-->
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:gcc">
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
5589e58b450c3d11010000772f742683f810741383f82b74153d10010000751cb8101100005dc3b8101000005dc3b82b2b00005dc3b8111100005dc331c05dc3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="dlgproc"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu dlgproc</com>
  <com>option switchselector on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option switchselector off</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #1: the re-rolled switch dispatches on the message parameter, and only the off pass re-rolls" min="1" max="1">switch\(a1\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #2: the case labels exist only in the pass that re-rolled" min="1" max="1">case 0x110:</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #3: with the option on the cascade survives as the compiler lowered it, over the same parameter" min="1" max="1">if \(a1 == 0x10\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #4: the switch never dispatches on a constant" min="0" max="0">switch\(0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #5: neither pass loses the dispatched-on parameter from the recovered prototype" min="0" max="0">dlgproc\(void\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #6: a case body is present in BOTH passes - the option changes the structure, not the content" min="2" max="2">return 0x2b2b;</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
